00:56.200 --> 01:06.540
So we were talking about getting a blow-off top from the lows before putting in a top, having a global bear market recession and slipping into a short period of asset price deflation into the four-year cycle lows.

01:06.760 --> 01:11.700
Whilst this thing went on longer than I thought it would, we're approaching extreme right translation in this four-year cycle.

01:12.020 --> 01:13.160
It is still in a blow-off top.

01:13.280 --> 01:16.960
We've managed to catch this thing in and out all the way up and we are still long and strong here.

01:17.160 --> 01:22.680
So one day at a time as always, but until this thing breaks down, then I think it's safe to say we're still in a blow-off top, right?

01:22.680 --> 01:28.920
Each leg is getting steeper and steeper into the top and at some point it is going to break and it's going to come down pretty hard and fast,

01:28.980 --> 01:35.280
just like we saw when we got the extreme right translation in the late teens leading up to that C19 plunge.

01:35.440 --> 01:37.300
So I suspect something like that will happen.

01:37.440 --> 01:41.320
Remember in terms of the recession back here, all of this happened in a blink of an eye.

01:41.600 --> 01:46.860
By the time we got here, the NBER, who's in charge of officially declaring and dating recessions, went back and said,

01:46.940 --> 01:48.660
yeah, I think that was a recession in there.

01:48.660 --> 01:55.480
So I do wonder if we'll get something like that, you know, a continuation of blow-off top, come slamming down to reset the four-year cycle low,

01:55.620 --> 01:59.980
and then by the time we get here somewhere in time, the NBER will say, yep, there's your recession.

02:00.400 --> 02:03.580
Certainly looks a lot like that based on some of the data I'll show you in a minute.

02:03.820 --> 02:08.140
The top could and should come imminently here, but as I've been saying, until something breaks,

02:08.180 --> 02:13.580
then I'm just going to continue to be extremely bullish because these parabolic advances when they occur do tend to steepen into the top.

02:13.780 --> 02:15.140
And to me, this doesn't look like a top yet.

02:15.140 --> 02:19.380
It looks like something with more legs to go, and the Nasdaq even closed at new all-time highs,

02:19.500 --> 02:22.360
so it's not something you typically see when we're about to nuke.

02:22.580 --> 02:25.620
It does look an awful lot to me like this is what we've got going on, right?

02:25.640 --> 02:31.000
We've had this kind of bear trap back here, this good shakeout, and now we're just advancing before going vertical.

02:31.260 --> 02:34.720
So like I said, I expect it to get steeper into the top, and so far it is.

02:35.120 --> 02:38.660
Meanwhile, Bitcoin is attempting to shake off its bear market decline.

02:38.880 --> 02:41.980
We know we've been trading these weekly cycles inside of the four-year cycles.

02:41.980 --> 02:46.280
Towards the top, we were looking at one or two paths, and of course, we've got this red path play out.

02:46.680 --> 02:51.420
In terms of time, we're right about here, which of course means, given that we've got higher high closes,

02:51.580 --> 02:57.880
the confidence in seeing a new low is waning, and it may well be exciting times ahead here for the Bitcoin trade

02:57.880 --> 03:04.260
because it looks to me like for the first time in a while, we're going to be able to start moving back to the bull market phase here,

03:04.260 --> 03:11.140
which is where we spend more time going up and less time consolidating, as opposed to more time coming down and less time consolidating.

03:11.320 --> 03:16.500
Unless we can top probably this week or next week and then really start to come down hard and fast,

03:16.580 --> 03:18.800
which would, of course, complete this yellow squiggle.

03:19.040 --> 03:22.280
Unless we can do something like this in the next week or two, right?

03:22.340 --> 03:24.120
Start to really spill down here.

03:24.120 --> 03:32.660
Then it does look to me like what we've got potentially is the start of a new regime where we're going to go up more than we come down, right?

03:32.740 --> 03:36.640
More in terms of distance traveled and also more in terms of time, right?

03:36.680 --> 03:41.980
So we may be able to start to see this unfold where we spend more time coming up, less time consolidating,

03:42.300 --> 03:44.440
more time coming up, less time consolidating.

03:44.920 --> 03:49.260
The distance between the weekly pivots should remain approximately equidistant,

03:49.260 --> 03:52.720
so that still means there will be a high probability tradable low in here.

03:52.880 --> 03:54.140
Same sort of timing window.

03:54.420 --> 03:58.040
It just means we might have a higher low instead of a lower low to buy.

03:58.220 --> 04:00.840
I'll leave this here unchanged for now.

04:00.960 --> 04:06.460
We'll give this thing about one to two more weeks just in case we get a big fat red candle that catches us back down

04:06.460 --> 04:10.860
in the kind of low 70s or high 60s within the next couple of weeks, okay?

04:10.860 --> 04:16.780
But if this continues to trend higher, then we'll just say, okay, no chance in hell we're getting a lower low here

04:16.780 --> 04:21.660
and instead we'll have to, like I said, adjust those boxes and start to look for a nice bullish uptrend,

04:21.900 --> 04:28.280
which I would personally love to see because buying every weekly cycle dip only to resolve to higher lows

04:28.280 --> 04:34.040
for me would be a much more fun environment to trade than what we've had over the last year or so.

04:34.200 --> 04:38.180
So the plan has always been to buy the low heavy once we get into this weekly cycle window.

04:38.180 --> 04:41.860
The question just remains whether that will be a higher low or a lower low.

04:42.160 --> 04:44.940
But in either case, it does not change the trade setup.

04:44.940 --> 04:48.940
Things start to get even more interesting for me here when we talk about the next part of this,

04:49.020 --> 04:52.520
which is that anything you do to the M2 rate of change shows up in the CPI with a lag.

04:52.880 --> 04:55.540
Because of the snapback we had after the C19 print,

04:55.620 --> 04:58.080
I've been talking about slipping into a short period of asset price deflation.

04:58.400 --> 04:59.920
It's a very highly contested call.

05:00.080 --> 05:02.620
People were looking at this and indeed asking questions, right?

05:02.660 --> 05:03.420
Did we bottom early?

05:03.480 --> 05:05.520
Are we not going to see continuation of disinflation?

05:05.720 --> 05:08.800
And a lot of this was driven, of course, by the short-term breakout in oil,

05:09.220 --> 05:11.300
which admittedly caught me a little bit off guard here.

05:11.300 --> 05:16.220
Although at least we did manage to call the top once we got the left translated weekly cycle.

05:16.340 --> 05:18.260
We've been talking about seeing something like this.

05:18.360 --> 05:19.740
And this is still very much in play.

05:19.840 --> 05:22.440
I know we've deviated away from the fractal, but it is only a fractal.

05:22.500 --> 05:23.660
It's never going to play out one-to-one.

05:23.820 --> 05:25.440
And I make the case that we're going to do something like this

05:25.440 --> 05:27.860
and come down into that cycle low as expected.

05:28.240 --> 05:29.240
And here's the key part though.

05:29.340 --> 05:31.560
Even if oil stays elevated here,

05:31.660 --> 05:33.300
we have elapsed enough time now

05:33.300 --> 05:36.680
where because we're measuring rate of change year over year,

05:36.680 --> 05:39.700
high oil prices can't even impact the CPI anymore

05:39.700 --> 05:42.280
because we're comparing here to here, right?

05:42.360 --> 05:43.940
Which is practically no change.

05:44.260 --> 05:46.320
So we're going to see lower CPI prints here,

05:46.400 --> 05:49.060
even if oil stays elevated because it's been a year

05:49.060 --> 05:50.980
and we're measuring relative to a year ago.

05:51.180 --> 05:53.380
And if this starts to drop, like I suspect it will,

05:53.440 --> 05:55.120
then you're going to see inflation come off sharply

05:55.120 --> 05:58.360
because we'll be measuring here relative to here a year ago.

05:58.440 --> 06:00.060
Okay. It's a big, big difference.

06:00.260 --> 06:02.160
The truflation has been ticking up

06:02.160 --> 06:05.520
despite having a couple of cold prints back to back on the headline data,

06:05.520 --> 06:08.960
but smooth it out, take core CPI inflation month over month

06:08.960 --> 06:10.760
and use three and six month averages.

06:11.020 --> 06:13.420
Okay. You can see that this thing is still disinflationary.

06:13.560 --> 06:15.280
Yes, there are short-term wobbles along the way,

06:15.420 --> 06:17.580
but we have not got a trend change yet.

06:17.840 --> 06:19.560
It's also true when we look at PCE,

06:19.760 --> 06:22.080
the truflation data has been ticking up a little bit,

06:22.200 --> 06:25.560
but the trimmed mean year over year, clearly disinflationary.

06:25.680 --> 06:28.800
Okay. It's still very reminiscent of this shape right here.

06:29.140 --> 06:33.640
Headline PCE three month average annualized below 2% now.

06:33.640 --> 06:35.900
And again, what does this shape look like to you?

06:36.080 --> 06:37.660
Okay. It looks like as expected,

06:37.800 --> 06:40.860
that probably was the secondary top to a lower high in inflation.

06:40.860 --> 06:43.520
And now the continuation of disinflation will continue.

06:43.660 --> 06:47.020
So people love to zoom in and pay attention to one or two wobbly prints.

06:47.140 --> 06:49.020
They love to talk about absolute inflation

06:49.020 --> 06:50.280
and ignore the fact on this channel,

06:50.340 --> 06:51.420
we're talking about rate of change,

06:51.420 --> 06:52.480
but here we are, okay,

06:52.500 --> 06:55.920
still in a disinflationary trend until something changes.

06:56.080 --> 06:59.140
And like I said, all of this has been driven by energy as we know,

06:59.340 --> 07:00.720
but energy has now reached a point

07:00.720 --> 07:06.080
where it's going to be incredibly difficult for energy to continue to sway the CPI basket here

07:06.080 --> 07:08.300
or the PCE or any of the inflation baskets.

07:08.640 --> 07:10.580
And we also had jobs data,

07:10.580 --> 07:13.120
which brings us onto the next component of this hypothesis,

07:13.120 --> 07:15.440
which is that we would have during the decline,

07:15.540 --> 07:17.780
during the correction into the four year cycle low,

07:17.880 --> 07:18.720
labor market deterioration.

07:19.540 --> 07:23.940
This week, we got US economy adding 29K jobs in September,

07:24.120 --> 07:25.720
well below the expectation of 89K.

07:26.540 --> 07:28.360
We got the unemployment rate tick up,

07:28.360 --> 07:29.560
which I'll show you the charts in a minute.

07:29.720 --> 07:33.380
And they even did more revisions downwards to August's job number.

07:33.620 --> 07:36.060
This marks the third weakest job report of 2026.

07:36.400 --> 07:38.000
And again, I keep making the case that

07:38.000 --> 07:39.720
if you look at all the prior recessions,

07:39.880 --> 07:42.900
okay, the labor market data looks identical.

07:43.200 --> 07:45.600
It's just the only thing that's missing is they haven't gone back,

07:45.980 --> 07:47.620
typically late, like they always are,

07:47.680 --> 07:50.100
and mark and paint those gray recessionary bars.

07:50.320 --> 07:54.380
But this looks exactly like the prior instances of recession.

07:54.860 --> 07:56.800
These are leading indicators, remember, okay?

07:56.800 --> 07:58.240
Same deal here and here and here.

07:58.500 --> 07:59.560
Temporary help services.

07:59.740 --> 08:00.820
Looks like it's bear flagging,

08:00.880 --> 08:02.760
getting ready to move even lower again, okay?

08:02.860 --> 08:05.400
Which, again, where's the gray recessionary bar here?

08:06.540 --> 08:06.900
Brr.

08:07.320 --> 08:07.660
Hello.

08:07.940 --> 08:08.880
You escaped from mama.

08:09.680 --> 08:10.140
Oh yeah.

08:10.420 --> 08:10.820
Oh yeah.

08:11.120 --> 08:13.420
Which, again, I keep making the case is really strange at the moment.

08:13.520 --> 08:15.020
We've got all this recessionary data.

08:15.140 --> 08:17.180
We've got this recessionary looks in the labor market.

08:17.280 --> 08:20.180
We've had consistent and persistent labor market deterioration.

08:20.340 --> 08:21.720
And we've had this in the face of

08:21.720 --> 08:24.180
constant and persistent downward revisions.

08:24.180 --> 08:26.980
We know they revised over a million jobs out of the data in 2025.

08:27.780 --> 08:29.500
They've revised over two million jobs

08:29.500 --> 08:31.900
out of the initially reported data in the last three years.

08:32.260 --> 08:33.360
And as I said a moment ago,

08:33.480 --> 08:35.820
they revised last month's print again.

08:36.300 --> 08:37.960
So it's a really weird situation

08:37.960 --> 08:40.380
because all of this is coupled with a rhetoric out there

08:40.380 --> 08:42.000
of a strong and resilient labor market

08:42.000 --> 08:44.240
and an inflation that is sticky narrative.

08:44.700 --> 08:47.020
It's very strange because none of that is actually true.

08:47.140 --> 08:48.780
And we can clearly see this in the data.

08:48.920 --> 08:51.140
We've got the unemployment rate ticked back up to 4.2%.

08:51.140 --> 08:54.120
Again, it doesn't take a genius to look at this data

08:54.120 --> 08:56.240
and say, what can we see with our eyes, right?

08:56.260 --> 08:58.780
What we can see is this is consistent and persistent

08:58.780 --> 09:00.040
ticking up from the lows.

09:00.300 --> 09:01.720
Yes, there's been pullbacks along the way,

09:01.940 --> 09:05.800
but it looks a lot like it is susceptible, at least, to a spike.

09:06.060 --> 09:06.880
And if it spikes,

09:07.120 --> 09:09.700
that would be synonymous with recessionary periods, okay?

09:09.720 --> 09:11.360
This is the only time in history so far

09:11.360 --> 09:14.060
we haven't had that lead-in resolve to a spike

09:14.060 --> 09:16.380
and then paint the recessionary bar over the top.

09:16.560 --> 09:19.040
When we look at things like the unemployment rate 16, 24-year-olds,

09:19.040 --> 09:21.300
again, for a few months here, it looked like

09:21.300 --> 09:22.540
we were starting to roll over.

09:22.680 --> 09:23.800
And on this channel, we were saying,

09:23.960 --> 09:26.440
if we can consistently, persistently observe

09:26.440 --> 09:29.420
improvement in the labor market data across the board,

09:29.500 --> 09:31.500
we could perhaps argue that we may get to swear

09:31.500 --> 09:33.520
in a recession here, which, of course, we all want

09:33.520 --> 09:34.980
because we don't want people to lose their jobs

09:34.980 --> 09:36.140
and their homes and everything else.

09:36.260 --> 09:37.880
But whilst we zoom in here, okay,

09:37.960 --> 09:39.540
look at this, at the hard right edge.

09:39.600 --> 09:41.780
It is now susceptible to the next leg higher.

09:41.980 --> 09:43.520
Doesn't mean we have to get one, okay?

09:43.600 --> 09:45.460
We could easily form a lower high right here

09:45.460 --> 09:47.220
and then head lower to lower lows.

09:47.220 --> 09:48.920
And then at this point, I would be saying, okay,

09:49.460 --> 09:51.920
looks like we're showing major signs of improvement here.

09:52.100 --> 09:52.680
Happy days.

09:52.820 --> 09:53.920
But the inverse is true.

09:54.060 --> 09:55.660
If this climbs to a higher high,

09:55.740 --> 09:57.580
it's going to look extraordinarily like

09:57.580 --> 10:00.040
we are heading for a recessionary period.

10:00.240 --> 10:02.800
And again, in hindsight, this will be super, super obvious

10:02.800 --> 10:05.560
because all of the data has looked recessionary for some time.

10:05.700 --> 10:08.800
All of the leading indicators look extremely recessionary.

10:08.900 --> 10:10.920
They're only absent of that bar that we know people

10:10.920 --> 10:13.780
like the NBER are always late to go back and paint.

10:14.020 --> 10:16.660
We'd be able to look at all the revisions made to the data

10:16.660 --> 10:17.580
and go, well, yeah.

10:17.800 --> 10:20.380
I mean, why would they keep quietly revising the data down?

10:20.620 --> 10:23.240
Why would they remove 2 million jobs over three years

10:23.240 --> 10:24.960
if everything was honky dory?

10:25.100 --> 10:27.400
All of this is going to seem extremely obvious in hindsight.

10:27.660 --> 10:28.860
When we look at this, it's like, yeah,

10:28.960 --> 10:29.840
this looks pretty good.

10:30.000 --> 10:32.460
And they've invented these strange narratives along the way.

10:32.760 --> 10:35.660
Like 4.1 is historically low unemployment, okay?

10:35.700 --> 10:36.380
But why?

10:36.500 --> 10:37.580
Because the Fed said so?

10:37.840 --> 10:38.800
It doesn't even make sense.

10:38.900 --> 10:40.640
Five out of the last 12 recessions

10:40.640 --> 10:43.240
started with the same or lower unemployment rate.

10:43.420 --> 10:44.460
So why are we pretending like

10:44.460 --> 10:46.140
this is historically low unemployment?

10:46.420 --> 10:48.340
Like, relative to what, right?

10:48.640 --> 10:50.620
Like, the whole thing is extremely weird.

10:50.920 --> 10:54.040
Meanwhile, the data is not really improving materially.

10:54.460 --> 10:57.560
U6, however, is starting to roll over more, okay?

10:57.620 --> 10:58.200
So that's good.

10:58.440 --> 11:00.940
And if this continues to trend down this way,

11:01.160 --> 11:02.720
that would be a point in the favour of saying,

11:02.860 --> 11:04.340
okay, we're not going to get a spike in unemployment,

11:04.440 --> 11:05.980
we're not going to have recessions, so on and so forth.

11:05.980 --> 11:07.640
But it's only going to take a couple more prints

11:07.640 --> 11:08.700
to be up at higher highs,

11:08.840 --> 11:10.640
and then it really is going to look an awful lot

11:10.640 --> 11:12.240
like those recessionary periods, okay?

11:12.240 --> 11:13.280
I'm just saying it's a risk.

11:13.380 --> 11:14.840
It's not a, the sky is falling,

11:14.980 --> 11:16.740
we all need to run for the hills or anything like that.

11:16.780 --> 11:18.700
I'm still buying equities, as you'll see in a moment.

11:18.940 --> 11:20.320
But what it is, is an acknowledgement

11:20.320 --> 11:23.160
that this is getting closer and closer by the day.

11:23.520 --> 11:24.600
And I think the bond market

11:24.600 --> 11:26.200
has kind of worked this out now, right?

11:26.220 --> 11:28.220
The bond market has been capitulating.

11:28.800 --> 11:29.980
Yields have been forced higher

11:29.980 --> 11:32.000
because of the capitulation in the bond market.

11:32.340 --> 11:34.480
The yields made it so high, in fact,

11:34.520 --> 11:35.940
that the Fed had to respond.

11:36.220 --> 11:39.060
It was basically being held hostage by the bond market,

11:39.060 --> 11:42.100
and it had to set the interest rate higher

11:42.100 --> 11:43.660
at the last FMMC meeting.

11:43.860 --> 11:46.840
But the bond market now has almost finished capitulating

11:46.840 --> 11:50.120
based on my sentiment read, based on my cycle analysis.

11:50.440 --> 11:51.840
And now the bond market is realizing,

11:52.040 --> 11:54.380
okay, we do continue to hold the power

11:54.380 --> 11:56.560
to hold the Fed hostage, right?

11:56.560 --> 11:57.880
We can continue to set rates.

11:57.940 --> 11:59.300
They're not going to do anything crazy,

11:59.300 --> 12:02.600
like allow us to have a 5.3, 10 year

12:02.600 --> 12:05.300
whilst holding rates significantly below that.

12:05.380 --> 12:06.320
And now, as expected,

12:06.320 --> 12:08.380
we're probably looking at one and done, okay?

12:08.500 --> 12:10.300
This is continuing to reprice this way.

12:10.540 --> 12:11.500
The yields should be topping,

12:11.800 --> 12:14.200
the bonds should be finishing capitulation imminently,

12:14.340 --> 12:16.140
and then we should even be back

12:16.140 --> 12:18.020
to a cutting regime in no time.

12:18.120 --> 12:20.820
And that is simply because there is no inflation.

12:21.100 --> 12:23.640
There is no strong and resilient labor market,

12:24.060 --> 12:26.400
and the growth is slowing in plenty of places.

12:26.840 --> 12:27.540
On top of that,

12:27.600 --> 12:29.720
the bond market has finished capitulating, okay?

12:29.800 --> 12:31.340
And institutions will now begin to rotate

12:31.340 --> 12:32.380
into those assets,

12:32.740 --> 12:34.860
just like we have been doing on this channel for some time.

12:34.860 --> 12:37.620
And a big bond bull lies ahead here,

12:37.700 --> 12:40.020
which I still maintain is the contrarian trade.

12:40.100 --> 12:42.760
And it's the contrarian trade for a couple of reasons, okay?

12:42.880 --> 12:44.960
Primarily because most people don't do

12:44.960 --> 12:46.680
any of the work I presented today.

12:46.780 --> 12:48.180
Most people don't look under the hood.

12:48.280 --> 12:49.760
They just look at a headline number,

12:49.980 --> 12:50.520
and they go,

12:50.720 --> 12:53.080
inflation is sticky, hyperinflation,

12:53.240 --> 12:54.740
whatever the hell is going on, okay?

12:55.060 --> 12:57.180
Rates to 11.9%.

12:57.180 --> 13:00.840
This is what people will be saying, right?

13:00.900 --> 13:03.040
But they don't actually really look at the data

13:03.040 --> 13:04.060
like we have done today.

13:04.140 --> 13:04.860
And when you do,

13:04.960 --> 13:06.400
it becomes extremely obvious

13:06.400 --> 13:09.520
that this whole thing has the market wrong-footed.

13:09.680 --> 13:12.180
The bond market is telling you for a reason, okay?

13:12.240 --> 13:14.880
Why does it want to have the interbank lending rate

13:14.880 --> 13:16.580
so far below the 10-year?

13:16.680 --> 13:18.620
It's because it knows the 10-year is about to reverse.

13:19.020 --> 13:19.980
What about the two-year?

13:20.200 --> 13:21.700
4.65%?

13:21.840 --> 13:23.500
It's because it knows the top is coming.

